      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "attrs": {
          "textAlign": "justify"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In our last lesson, we explored the world of conjunctions, examining coordinating (FANBOYS), subordinating (because, although, if, when, etc.), and correlative (both...and, either...or, etc.) types. We focused on their functions in connecting ideas, the specific grammatical structures and punctuation they require, and common errors for Brazilian learners, such as comma usage and distinguishing between conjunctions with similar meanings but different structures (e.g., but vs. although)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Quick Practice:",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "attrs": {
          "start": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine these sentences using a subordinating conjunction: \"He felt ill. He went to work.\""
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "What is the difference in punctuation when a dependent clause starts a sentence versus when it ends it?"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Correct the error: \"Neither the manager or the employees were happy.\""
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "horizontalRule"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "🎯 Overview",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "attrs": {
          "textAlign": "justify"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Slang is a type of language consisting of very informal words and phrases, more common in speech than writing, and often restricted to a particular group or context. It changes constantly, varies greatly across regions (e.g., American vs. British vs. Australian English), and is used to express familiarity, identity, or cultural connection."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "attrs": {
          "textAlign": "justify"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Understanding slang helps C1 learners interpret informal conversations, TV shows, movies, and songs. While using slang can sound natural in casual settings, it's important to use it cautiously to avoid sounding unnatural, outdated, or inappropriate."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "attrs": {
          "textAlign": "justify"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This lesson will introduce common slang terms (especially American and British), categorize them (greetings, emotions, reactions, etc.), provide context, and give guidance on when and how to use them safely."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "horizontalRule"
      },
